ement in [the mutation-heavy loop]([REDACTED:path]).\n\n3. **Keep remote correspondence and target integration as separate analysis facts.**  \n   [`LocalCommitRelation`]([REDACTED:path]) makes `LocalAndRemote` and `Integrated` mutually exclusive. In reality, a commit can have a remote counterpart *and* be integrated into the target.\n\n   Keeping those facts separately during analysis would make the precedence explicit when constructing the existing display enum. Gerrit could supply the remote correspondence without subsequently rewriting completed commits. These facts should remain scoped to the relevant segment, since the remote relationship depends on the branch.\n\n4. **Calculate push status once, after resolving the facts.**  \n   Currently a segment starts with `NothingToPush`, similarity calculates its status, and Gerrit can calculate it again. The constructor could instead resolve commits and then choose the ordinary-Git or Gerrit status calculation.\n\n   There is a deeper model issue here too: `PushStatus::Integrated` combines target integration with local/remote push state. Keeping those inputs distinct internally would clarify the rules while preserving the existing API enum. See [segment construction]([REDACTED:path]).\n\n5. **Make review association return a value.**  \n   [`apply_review_to_metadata`]([REDACTED:path]) already computes the selected review first, then mutates optional metadata, sometimes creating metadata solely to hold that review.\n\n   The small refactor is to return the projected metadata. The larger model simplification would separate stored branch metadata from the resolved review association. That would eliminate synthetic metadata, but would require changing consumers. The anonymous `(number, bool, optional head)` review tuple also deserves a named type.\n\nI’d start with the unused `target_commit` field, then the squash-boundary value, then make the stack constructor produce finished segments.\n\nThe practical target is **analysis that returns facts, followed by a mapping into `RefInfo`**. Squash detection spans a stack, and expensive similarity checks share a time budget, so some stateful computation remains useful. Also, remote pruning currently happens before squash and Gerrit enrichment; changing that ordering needs separate behavioral verification.\n\nI haven’t changed any files or run tests; these conclusions come from the current source and branch history.","tool_name":null,"tool_input":null,"source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T19:18:10.132Z","ordinal":130,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","role":"assistant","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","create_time":1788895042.264593,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]},"phase":"final_answer"}}}